WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.040
While It brought some much-needed life back
to the box office after a disappointing summer,

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:11.880
Darren Aronofsky's Mother is the first casualty
of the fall after opening to just $7.5 million.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.300
The hard-to-define film stars Jennifer Lawrence
as the title character, wife to Javier Bardem's

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.680
"Him", as a woman who must deal with the
increasing stress of unwanted visitors entering

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.060
her once-peaceful home.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.600
The film's vague log line has been a topic
of discussion for months, and the movie became

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:33.040
even more shrouded in mystery as confusing
trailers and TV spots hit the airwaves.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.300
While Mother was as hard to track as it was
to explain, no one expected the film to fall

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.220
as far as it did.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.480
Let's take a look at why Mother has had trouble
finding an audience.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:43.440
Critical division

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:47.740
While the Rotten Tomatoes score on Mother
isn't terrible, critics were certainly divided

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.460
over how good … or bad Mother is.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.039
Some reviewers hailed the film for its visual
brilliance, while others were left confused

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.440
and frustrated, calling the pic pretentious
and nonsensical.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:00:59.940
"I don't like it that people booed.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.900
I'm not a masochist, but I want a reaction
from people."

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.420
Although Aronofsky's movies have been widely
respected, and even his best works are hard

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.720
to watch twice, Mother reviews indicated it
might be hard to watch even once.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.560
Some accused the writer-director of trying
to mix in too much allegory and extremism

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.700
to the film to endure.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.200
Others thought it was bleak just for the sake
of it.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.240
And almost everyone agreed that a screening
of Mother is an intense, bewildering, and

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.460
disturbing experience.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:30.580
Moviegoers obviously enjoyed the light thrill
of seeing a movie like It, where the scares

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:31.880
are comparatively wholesome.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:36.900
But audiences obviously weren't as interested
in traveling down another of Aronofsky's existential

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:37.980
wormholes this weekend.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.100
And even those who did see it were unimpressed.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.300
It was the opening night audience that earned
the film the rare distinction of an F rating

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.320
on CinemaScore.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.520
Mother isn't the first film from a director
to get the dreaded rating.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:54.220
Steven Soderbergh's Solaris also holds that
distinction, so some consider it a badge of

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:54.720
honor.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.180
But it's still surprising to see a film with
a director and actors of this caliber be so

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.420
thoroughly docked by audiences.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.340
Mixed marketing

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.190
Given its difficult premise, almost no one
goes into Mother really knowing what it's

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:09.180
about in advance.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.580
Part of the reason for that is reviewers have
had such a tough time explaining what happened

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.099
even after seeing the movie.

00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:19.280
And despite Google searches for explanation
spiking after the film's release, the results

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.660
for such queries can be just as confusing
as the movie itself.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.620
Audiences may often enjoy twisty films, but
the biggest problem with Mother seems to be

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.640
that no one can agree on the true meaning.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.800
Is this all just a cautionary tale on climate
change?

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.000
Is the title character really just Mother
Earth who's being abused by Javier Bardem's

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.230
God/Devil hybrid and all the terrible people
that flock to him?

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:44.090
Or is it a parable about the Bible's creation
story, with Jennifer Lawrence's character

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:45.280
as some kind of Eve?

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:46.820
No one can be quite sure.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.960
"We are all living on this planet with this
one mother who gave us life."

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.780
Given that Aronofsky's last movie Noah was
based on the bible and did well in the international

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:01.050
box office, it might've seemed like a good
idea to continue on the theological train.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:05.180
But the fact that no one's quite sure what
Mother is trying to represent makes it a very

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.319
difficult sell.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:07.880
Bait and switch

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:12.480
While Jennifer Lawrence's fans might've been
shocked by seeing her ditch that defiant persona

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.780
that has informed so much of her career so
far, general audiences were probably more

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.540
surprised by how little horror the movie contains.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:23.300
The original teasers for Mother made the film
seem like a horror movie, which is what earned

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.530
some serious interest prior to release.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:29.640
The film's trailer drew out some of the film's
biggest scares, using the sounds of a ticking

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:34.100
clock to underscore what seemed to be a psychologically
focused haunted house movie.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.380
Even Aronofsky himself insisted it was meant
to be a scare.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:43.680
"It began with me wanting to get back to the
horror genre I did in Black Swan."

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.700
However, in the end, that genre identification
effort backfired, because Mother is definitely

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.640
not your usual Halloween fare.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:56.080
Instead, the movie delves into the human psyche,
albeit with some very freaky imagery.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:59.800
While those who were familiar with Aronofsky's
prior work might not have been surprised by

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:04.230
that, those who were just following the cues
left in the film's marketing were likely fairly

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.940
turned off by what they saw.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:07.620
Stiff competition

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.890
Choosing the perfect release date can be all-important
to a film's success, and studios will occasionally

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:15.800
shift around titles based on what other companies
are scheduling.

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:20.100
This was the case with Mother, which saw its
planned October release moved up a month to

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.160
avoid competing with Happy Death Day.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.360
And while that might've saved them from going
up against that movie's crowd, it did end

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:30.630
up pitting the pic against Stephen King's
It, which defied all expectations at the box

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:33.980
office and continued to draw in audiences
for its second weekend.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:38.371
Rather than trying out something they didn't
understand and no one seemed to like, moviegoers

00:04:38.371 --> 00:04:42.260
seemed to take the safe bet for their weekend
thrills by hitting up It, which had a lot

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:43.780
more word of mouth buzz.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:46.040
Wide release washout

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:50.720
Despite the big stars attached, Mother is
ultimately an arthouse film, and one that

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.920
really isn't designed for a wide audience.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:56.440
Paramount Pictures might've been looking at
the potential dollar signs when putting Mother

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:00.780
out to more than 2,300 theaters right away,
but in hindsight, it might've fared better

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:02.700
by seeking limited release first.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:06.660
The strategy to put a niche film on a limited
theatrical run first before expanding was

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:11.120
exactly what worked for Aronofsky's Black
Swan, which was similarly eerie and stirred

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.760
up its fair share of audience confusion at
the end, too.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:17.760
But because of the cultural must-see moment
that was produced by Natalie Portman's performance

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:21.690
in the pic, enough buzz justified getting
it into more theaters and the movie turned

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:23.180
a nice profit as a result.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:28.240
Perhaps a slower roll-out could've saved Mother
from being lashed at by audiences right away

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.680
to kill any momentum the movie might've earned
by creating such a conversation among critics.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:33.940
Thanks for watching!

